g on single‑event symbolism for his own struggle.\n- **Empathy for repression**: Having witnessed British crackdowns in Burma, he would empathise with Gandhi’s plight, deepening his resentment toward colonial rule.\n\n**Motivational shift** –\n- **Hybrid strategy**: While still inspired by Gandhi’s moral philosophy, Aung San would be more inclined to blend non‑violent protest with covert political organisation (e.g., underground networks, armed training) to guard against the possibility that a single protest could be pre‑empted.\n- **Regional solidarity**: He would seek closer ties with other anti‑colonial leaders (e.g., Ho Chi Minh, Sukarno) to create a pan‑Asian front that could not be easily disabled by a single colonial authority.\n\n**Strategic adaptation** –\n- **Emphasis on mass education**: Aung San would launch a campaign to raise political consciousness among Burmese peasants, emphasizing self‑reliance and local governance, thus creating a grassroots base that is less vulnerable to top‑down suppression.\n- **Contingency planning**: He would develop secret cells that could continue the struggle if a leader is arrested, ensuring the movement’s continuity regardless of who is targeted.\n\n### Synthesis\nOverall, the removal of the Salt March would push Gandhi toward a more decentralised, education‑focused non‑violent movement; it would embolden Churchill to double‑down on authoritarian tactics while fearing unforeseen manipulations; and it would cause Aung San to adopt a more cautious, hybrid approach that mixes moral non‑violence with pragmatic contingency measures. Each figure’s mental state – shock, vindication, disappointment – directly informs the shift in their political calculus, illustrating how a single temporal intervention can cascade through the motivations and strategies of leaders across continents. --> Text length need to be between 0 and 5000 characters

