**“Money Matters: Introducing Financial Literacy to High‑School Students”**  

---

## 1. Learning Objectives  
By the end of the workshop teachers will be able to:

1. **Explain** the basic concepts of budgeting, credit, saving, and consumer protection.  
2. **Design** a short classroom lesson (15‑20 min) that lets students create a simple personal budget.  
3. **Facilitate** at least one interactive activity that helps students see how credit scores or interest work.  
4. **Choose** appropriate free resources (videos, worksheets, calculators) to support each lesson.  
5. **Assess** student understanding through quick formative checks and a final reflective task.

---

## 2. Logical Sequence of Topics (≈ 3 hours total)

| Time | Segment | Core Content |
|------|----------|--------------|
| 0‑10 min | **Welcome & Ice‑breaker** | Quick “What money topic confuses you most?” poll (show results on board). |
| 10‑30 min | **Why Financial Literacy Matters** | Brief research‑based stats on teen money habits; link to curriculum standards. |
| 30‑55 min | **Topic 1 – Budgeting Basics** | Income vs. expenses, the 50/30/20 rule, introduction to a simple spreadsheet template. |
| 55‑70 min | **Activity 1 – Personal‑Budget Simulation** | Small groups receive a mock paycheck and a list of expenses; they allocate money and discuss trade‑offs. |
| 70‑90 min | **Topic 2 – Credit & Debt** | What a credit score is, APR vs. interest, common loan types, pitfalls of high‑interest credit cards. |
| 90‑105 min | **Activity 2 – Credit‑Score Role Play** | Pairs act as “Lender” and “Borrower”; lender reviews a mock credit report and decides on loan terms. |
| 105‑125 min | **Topic 3 – Saving & Investing Overview** | Savings accounts, CDs, basic idea of stocks/ETFs, power of compound interest (demo calculator). |
| 125‑140 min | **Activity 3 – Mini‑Investment Game** | Using a free online simulator, each group distributes a $10 000 virtual portfolio across three low‑risk assets and predicts growth. |
| 140‑155 min | **Assessment Strategies** | Quick quiz ideas, rubric sketch for a budgeting project, exit‑ticket prompt. |
| 155‑180 min | **Resources & Next Steps** | Hand out curated list, discuss how to adapt the workshop for a 6‑week unit or a single class. |

*(Times are flexible; the whole plan can be compressed into a single 90‑minute session by selecting only the first two topics and activities.)*

---

## 3. Interactive Classroom Activities (at least three)

1. **Personal‑Budget Simulation** – Groups work with a printable pay‑stub, assign funds to categories, then present the toughest trade‑off they faced.  
2. **Credit‑Score Role Play** – Learners examine a simplified credit report (provided worksheet) and negotiate loan terms, reinforcing fairness and risk concepts.  
3. **Mini‑Investment Game** – Using the free **Investopedia Stock Simulator**, teams allocate virtual money, record projected returns, and discuss diversification.  

*All activities include a brief debrief (2‑3 min) where the teacher guides students to connect the experience to real‑world decisions.*

---

## 4. Assessment Methods

| Method | When to Use | What It Measures |
|--------|------------|------------------|
| **Formative Quiz** (5‑10 multiple‑choice questions) | After each topic (via Google Forms or paper) | Immediate recall of key terms & concepts |
| **Budget Project Rubric** | End of unit (or at the end of the workshop if time‑pressed) | Completeness, realistic assumptions, reflection on choices |
| **Exit Ticket** (one‑sentence summary) | End of each class | Student’s personal “aha” moment |
| **Peer Review Checklist** | After the budget activity | Ability to give constructive feedback on peers’ budgets |

The rubric and checklist can be printed or shared digitally; they are simple enough for teachers to adapt quickly.

---

## 5. Curated List of Free Resources  

| Resource | Type | Link |
|----------|------|------|
| **Khan Academy – Personal Finance** | Short videos + practice exercises | https://www.khanacademy.org/college-careers-more/personal-finance |
| **MyMoney.gov – Budget Calculator** | Interactive budgeting tool (also printable worksheet) | https://www.mymoney.gov/tools/budget-calculator |
| **Federal Trade Commission – Consumer Information** | Guides on fraud, identity theft, and consumer rights | https://www.consumer.ftc.gov/ |
| **Investopedia Stock Simulator** | Free market‑simulation platform | https://www.investopedia.com/simulator/ |
| **Google Sheets Budget Template** | Editable spreadsheet (copy‑and‑use) | https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1XYZ... |
| **Financial Literacy Worksheets (PDF)** – grades 9‑12 | Printable activities on budgeting, credit, saving | https://www.mymoney.gov/sites/default/files/HighSchool_FinLit.pdf |

*(Teachers can download the PDFs ahead of time and upload the links to their LMS for easy student access.)*

---

## 6. Quick “Starter Kit” for Teachers  

1. **Print** the three activity worksheets (budget, credit report, investment sheet).  
2. **Create** a Google Form with 5‑question quiz (one per topic).  
3. **Copy** the Google Sheets budget template into a class folder.  
4. **Bookmark** the two video playlists (Khan Academy, FTC) for optional flip‑classroom use.  

With these items ready, teachers can launch the unit within a week, adapting the depth of each lesson to their class schedule.

---

### Closing Note  
This plan gives a solid framework—clear objectives, a logical flow of topics, three hands‑on activities, practical assessment ideas, and a ready‑to‑use set of free resources. Teachers can expand any section (e.g., add a guest speaker on credit cards) or trim it to fit a single class period, making the workshop both usable and flexible. --> Text length need to be between 0 and 5000 characters

