3. **Negotiate use agreement** – lease or “use‑as‑public‑space” contract with the city, property owner, or school district. Obtain liability insurance and any required permits (zoning, health).  

*Deliverable:* Signed site‑use agreement + site‑audit report.

---

## 3. Community Outreach & Volunteer Recruitment (Month 3‑4)

| Activity | Details | Tools |
|----------|---------|-------|
| Town‑hall meeting | Present the garden idea, gather crop preferences, address concerns. Hold at a local community center or via Zoom. | Presentation deck, sign‑up sheet |
| Volunteer hub | Set up a simple Google Form or use SignUpGenius; offer shifts (Sat am, Sun pm, weekday evenings). | Online form, printed flyers |
| Partner network | Connect with nearby schools, churches, senior centers, and the local food bank for volunteer pipelines and future distribution partners. | Email outreach, partnership agreements |
| Social‑media push | Create a dedicated Instagram/Facebook page; post weekly updates, “Meet the volunteer” spotlights, and progress photos. | Free scheduling tools (Later, Buffer) |

*Goal:* Enlist at least 30 regular volunteers and 3 partner organizations before ground‑break.

---

## 4. Resource Acquisition & Site Preparation (Month 4‑5)

1. **Materials list** – raised‑bed kits (or reclaimed lumber), compost, basic hand tools, drip‑irrigation kit, mulch, signage, small tool‑shed.  
2. **Solicit donations** – approach local hardware stores, garden centers, and a nearby landscaping firm for in‑kind gifts. Offer acknowledgment on signage and social media.  
3. **Soil preparation** – clear debris, add a 2‑inch layer of compost or compost‑rich soil, and lightly till with volunteers (use hand tools or a rented rototiller for larger beds).  
4. **Install infrastructure** –  
   * Build raised beds (4‑ft × 8‑ft) and a perimeter fence.  
   * Set up drip‑line irrigation (connect to a municipal spigot or rain‑barrel system).  
   * Place a modest tool‑shed and compost bin.  

*Timeline:* 2 weeks of volunteer work (one “Prep Day” plus a follow‑up “Build Day”).

---

## 5. Planting & Early Growth (Month 5‑7)

| Phase | Action | Notes |
|-------|--------|-------|
| Crop selection | Choose fast‑growing, nutrient‑dense vegetables (lettuce, kale, radish, bush beans, cherry tomatoes) and at least two culturally relevant crops based on community input. | Aim for 60 % of beds in “quick‑harvest” varieties. |
| Planting calendar | Create a simple 3‑month calendar: <br>• Late May – sow lettuce & radish (direct‑seed). <br>• Early June – transplant tomato seedlings. <br>• Mid June – plant beans. | Stagger plantings to keep a steady harvest. |
| Planting days | Organize two volunteer planting events (one for seed sowing, one for transplanting). Provide a short “how‑to” handout (seed depth, spacing, watering). | Assign a lead volunteer to each bed. |

---

## 6. Ongoing Maintenance & Education (Month 7‑12)

1. **Maintenance roster** – Weekly volunteer shifts (2‑hour blocks) covering: watering (check drip lines), weeding, pest scouting, and harvesting. Rotate teams to avoid burnout.  
2. **Monthly workshops** – Topics such as compost basics, kitchen cooking with garden produce, and simple pest‑control methods. Host at the garden or partner community center.  
3. **Harvest distribution** – Set up a “pick‑your‑own” stand on Saturdays; surplus goes to the partner food bank. Keep a simple log of how many households receive produce each week.  

---

## 7. Impact Measurement & Reporting (Quarterly)

| Metric | How to Track | Frequency |
|--------|--------------|-----------|
| Produce weight | Use a kitchen scale to record total pounds harvested each week. | Weekly |
| Households served | Count boxes or bags handed out; maintain a sign‑in sheet at the stand. | Weekly |
| Volunteer hours | Volunteers log time on a shared Google Sheet. | Ongoing |
| Workshop attendance | Sign‑in sheets at each session. | Monthly |
| Community feedback | Short paper survey (3‑question) distributed quarterly. | Quarterly |

*Reporting:* Compile a one‑page summary every three months (metrics + photos + a short story) and share with funders, partners, and the neighborhood newsletter.

---

## 8. Next Steps & Scaling (End of Year 1)

* Review the impact data and volunteer feedback.  
* Identify any gaps (e.g., water supply, additional beds).  
* Prepare a modest expansion proposal (add a vertical‑tower garden or a second site) and seek additional funding.  

---

### Quick‑Reference Checklist (Print‑out for the Committee)

- [ ] Mission & metrics defined  
- [ ] Steering committee formed  
- [ ] Funding applications submitted  
- [ ] Site selected & lease signed  
- [ ] Soil test completed  
- [ ] Community town‑hall held  
- [ ] Volunteer sign‑up platform live  
- [ ] Material donations secured  
- [ ] Beds built & irrigation installed  
- [ ] Planting calendar posted  
- [ ] Maintenance roster posted  
- [ ] First harvest distribution launched  
- [ ] Quarterly impact report template ready  

Following this roadmap should give your nonprofit a realistic, actionable path to launch a volunteer‑driven garden that delivers fresh produce to low‑income neighborhoods while building community ownership and measurable impact. Good luck! --> Text length need to be between 0 and 5000 characters

