## Week 1 – Survey & Soil Prep (Days 1‑7)

| Day | Action | Why it matters |
|-----|--------|----------------|
| 1 | **Measure & Sketch** – Use a tape measure to note the dimensions of the space you’ll work with (e.g., 6 × 8 ft). Draw a quick layout on paper or a free phone app, marking the sunniest spots (≥ 6 h direct sun). | Pollinators need sun; a sketch helps you place plants with compatible spacing. |
| 2‑3 | **Choose 5‑6 Low‑Cost Native Plants** – Pick species that bloom at different times. Good budget picks (bare‑root, seed packets, or small container plants) include: <br>• *Echinacea purpurea* (purple coneflower) – bees & butterflies <br>• *Asclepias tuberosa* (butterfly milkweed) – monarchs <br>• *Salvia ‘Hot Lips’* or *Salvia nemorosa* – hummingbirds <br>• *Coreopsis tinctoria* – long‑season nectar <br>• *Monarda ‘Jacob’s Ladder’* – all three groups | Staggered bloom gives food throughout the season. |
| 4 | **Cheap Soil Test** – Purchase a basic pH/nutrient kit (≈ $5) or use a home‑brew vinegar test. Record results. | Knowing pH lets you amend only what’s needed. |
| 5‑6 | **Amend the Soil** – Spread a 1‑2 in layer of compost (often free from municipal drop‑off sites) and, if pH is low, sprinkle a small amount of garden lime (≈ $3). Lightly rake in with a spade. | Improves drainage, nutrient availability and root health. |
| 7 | **Mulch Base** – Collect straw, shredded leaves, or newspaper (free from yard‑waste programs). Lay 2‑3 in thick around the planting area. | Retains moisture, suppresses weeds and keeps costs down. |

---

## Week 2 – Planting (Days 8‑14)

| Day | Action | Tips |
|-----|--------|------|
| 8 | **Mark Planting Spots** – Use a stick or garden marker to outline each plant’s location per your sketch. Space most perennials 12‑18 in apart; taller *Salvia* in the back. | Keeps the garden tidy and gives each plant room to grow. |
| 9‑10 | **Plant** – Dig holes twice the width of the root ball, set the plant at the same depth it was in the pot, backfill with native soil, and firm gently. Water in each hole after planting. | Proper planting reduces transplant shock. |
| 11 | **Deep Water** – Give the whole bed a thorough soak (≈ 10 min) with a watering can or hose. | Helps roots settle. |
| 12‑13 | **Optional DIY Drip** – If you have a ½‑in garden hose, lay it in a loose “U” shape across the bed, poke a few tiny holes with a nail, then cover with mulch. Set a timer (≈ $12 on a discount site) to run 15 min twice a week. | Saves water and time; you can skip it if you prefer hand‑watering. |
| 14 | **Hummingbird Water Spot** – Place a shallow saucer or dish with a stone in the center of the garden, fill with fresh water, and change daily. | Provides a quick drink source for hummingbirds. |

---

## Week 3 – Maintenance & Attractants (Days 15‑21)

| Day | Action | Details |
|-----|--------|--------|
| 15‑21 (every 2 days) | **Weed & Mulch Check** – Pull any new weeds and top‑up mulch where it looks thin. | Keeps competition low and moisture steady. |
| 16 | **Light Fertilizer** – Apply a handful of balanced organic fertilizer (e.g., 10‑10‑10) at half the label rate around the base of each plant. | Supports early growth without encouraging excessive foliage. |
| 18 | **Simple Bee Hotel** – Collect a few 4‑in lengths of untreated bamboo or a drilled wooden block, bundle with twine, and stake in a sunny corner. | Gives solitary bees nesting sites for free. |
| 19 | **Butterfly Rocks** – Place a couple of flat stones in sunny spots; butterflies use them to warm up. | No cost, easy to do. |
| 20‑21 | **Pest Spotting** – Look for aphids, spider mites, or slugs. If found, spray a mild soap solution (1 tsp dish soap per quart water) and repeat after 3 days. | Safe for pollinators once the spray dries. |

---

## Week 4 – Observation, Tweaking & Sharing (Days 22‑28)

| Day | Action | How it helps |
|-----|--------|--------------|
| 22‑23 | **Pollinator Survey** – Spend 5‑10 min each morning watching the garden. Note which insects visit which plants (a simple notebook or phone note works). | Gives you feedback on which plants are most attractive. |
| 24 | **Adjust Watering** – Check soil moisture 2‑3 in down; if dry, water a bit more; if soggy, reduce frequency. | Prevents over‑ or under‑watering. |
| 25‑26 | **Dead‑Head & Trim** – Clip spent blooms on *Echinacea*, *Coreopsis* and *Salvia* to encourage a second flush of flowers. | Extends nectar availability. |
| 27 | **Show Off** – Post a quick photo and a short “what’s visiting” update on a neighborhood Facebook group or Nextdoor. Invite friends to stop by. | More eyes = more pollinator traffic (they follow each other). |
| 28 | **Plan Next Season** – Write down which plants performed best and consider adding another of those next year. | Builds on success and makes future gardening easier. |

---

## Rough Budget (All items can be sourced cheaply or for free)

| Item | Approx. Cost |
|------|---------------|
| Plants (bare‑root or small containers) | $30‑$45 |
| Compost (bulk bag) | $10 |
| Lime (small bag) | $3 |
| Mulch (straw/leaves) | Free‑$5 |
| Soil test kit | $5 |
| Optional hose timer | $12 |
| Bee‑hotel materials (bamboo or wood) | $8 |
| Soap & fertilizer | $5 |
| **Total** | **$73‑$93** |

---

### Why this plan works
* **Time‑bound:** Each week has a clear focus, keeping you on track to finish within 4 weeks.  
* **Budget‑friendly:** Uses free or low‑cost materials and selects inexpensive plant options.  
* **Pollinator‑centric:** Native, nectar‑rich plants, water source, and nesting habitats address the needs of bees, butterflies and hummingbirds.  
* **Maintainable:** Simple, repeatable tasks (weeding, watering, occasional pest spray) that fit a beginner’s schedule and tools.

Follow the checklist, adjust as you learn what visits your garden, and you’ll have a thriving, pollinator‑friendly oasis in just one month. Happy gardening! --> Text length need to be between 0 and 5000 characters

