| **Geopolitical asymmetries** | Nations with advanced biotech capacity (US, China, EU) dominate research funding; low‑income countries contribute little to data but may face imported risks (e.g., accidental release of a gene‑drive organism). | *Global justice*: international agreements should obligate technology‑rich states to share benefits, capacity‑build, and assume liability for transboundary harms. |

*Take‑away*: Ethical frameworks must embed equity mechanisms—public financing, affordable pricing, and global governance—to prevent a genetic “underclass” and ensure benefits are shared broadly.

