| **Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) with a tiered fee structure** | Manufacturers pay a fee proportional to the recyclability and carbon intensity of their packaging; funds are earmarked for collection and recycling. | Creates a direct economic incentive to redesign products for reuse or for easier recycling, while generating a stable financing stream for waste‑management systems. | Start with a pilot covering food‑service packaging; use existing life‑cycle‑assessment (LCA) data to set tier thresholds. |
| **Targeted bans or fees on the most problematic items** (e.g., lightweight bags, straws, expanded polystyrene containers). | Removes the cheapest, highest‑impact plastics from the market or makes them costly enough that alternatives become competitive. | Evidence from several EU and Asian jurisdictions shows rapid substitution when price signals are strong. | Phase‑in over 2‑3 years, allowing time for industry to develop compliant alternatives; pair with a “green‑label” certification for compliant products. |
| **Public‑procurement standards for reusable or refillable systems** | Government agencies (schools, hospitals, municipal cafeterias) must purchase only reusable containers or refill stations. | The public sector is a large, predictable buyer; its demand can drive market scale‑up of reusable solutions. | Publish a model procurement clause and provide a transition grant for small suppliers to adapt. |
| **Incentivising design for recyclability** (e.g., mandatory mono‑material packaging, standardized labelling). | Reduces contamination in the recycling stream and simplifies material recovery. | Cleaner feedstock commands higher market prices and lowers processing costs. | Align labelling with the existing Resin Identification Code (RIC) system and require a “recyclable‑by‑2025” declaration on all new products. |

**2. Strengthening Recycling Infrastructure and Boosting Public Participation**

| Intervention | Expected impact | Key actions |
|---|---|---|
| **Modernise Material‑Recovery Facilities (MRFs)** – invest in optical sorters, AI‑driven contamination detection, and equipment capable of handling a broader range of resin types. | Higher recovery rates (10‑15 % increase in usable recycled plastic) and lower contamination, making recycled feedstock more attractive to manufacturers. | Allocate capital grants tied to performance targets; require public‑private partnerships to share risk and expertise. |
| **Standardised, colour‑coded collection system** (e.g., blue for PET, green for HDPE, orange for mixed plastics) combined with clear, front‑of‑pack labelling. | Reduces sorting errors at the household level, lowering downstream contamination. | Run a national branding campaign (similar to “Recycle Right”) and provide municipalities with a starter kit of bins. |
| **Deposit‑Return Schemes (DRS) for beverage containers** (minimum 10 ¢ per container). | Proven to lift return rates to >80 % in Nordic countries; creates a direct financial incentive for consumers. | Legislate a universal DRS covering all single‑use beverage containers, with a streamlined reverse‑logistics network managed by an independent operator. |
| **Behavioural outreach and school‑based curricula** that explain the environmental and economic benefits of proper sorting, plus “plastic‑free challenges” for communities. | Improves participation rates, especially in demographics with historically low recycling engagement. | Partner with NGOs to develop age‑appropriate modules; evaluate impact through pre‑/post‑surveys. |
| **Market creation for recycled plastics** – set government procurement targets (e.g., 30 % recycled content in all public‑sector plastic purchases) and offer tax credits to companies that use recycled resin. | Guarantees demand, stabilising prices for recyclates and encouraging investment in collection and processing. | Publish a transparent “recycled‑content dashboard” to track compliance; adjust credit levels annually based on market conditions. |

**3. Coordinated Governance**

1. **National Plastic Action Plan** – a single legislative framework that links the above measures, sets clear quantitative targets (e.g., 50 % reduction in single‑use plastic sales by 2030, 70 % recycling rate by 2035), and assigns a lead agency for monitoring.
