Marketing Manager”, “Head of Brand”, or “Creative Director”. Export their names and emails.\n3. **Cold‑Email Template** – Write a 3‑sentence email:\n   *Subject:* “Elevating [Company]’s brand identity”\n   *Body:* Brief intro, mention a specific pain point you observed (e.g., "Your recent blog series lacks a visual hierarchy"), attach one‑pager, request a 15‑minute call.\n4. **Send 15 Emails per Day** – Use Mailshake or a Gmail extension to schedule and track opens.\n\n### Week 4 – Content & Authority Building\n1. **Publish a Mini‑Case Study Blog** – Write a 800‑word post about a local project that increased client engagement by X %. Share on LinkedIn and Medium.\n2. **Start a Weekly “Design Insight” Newsletter** – 200‑word tip for B2B branding. Collect sign‑ups through your website (use Mailchimp free tier).\n3. **Guest Post / Podcast** – Pitch a 5‑minute design insight to a niche industry newsletter or podcast (e.g., “Design for Fintech”).\n\n### Weeks 5‑6 – Follow‑up & Networking\n1. **Follow‑up Emails** – For any unopened cold mails, resend with a different subject line. For opened mails, send a brief “just checking in” after 3 days.\n2. **Attend Virtual Industry Events** – Register for at least two webinars or meet‑ups in your niche. Engage in chat, offer a free 30‑minute audit to participants.\n3. **Leverage Existing Clients** – Ask satisfied café owners for introductions to corporate contacts they know. Offer a modest referral fee ($200) to incentivize.\n\n### Weeks 7‑8 – Proposal & Pricing Optimization\n1. **Standard Proposal Template** – Include scope, milestones, deliverables, and a line‑item price table. Keep it under 2 pages.\n2. **Value‑Based Pricing** – Instead of hourly rates, propose flat‑fee packages (e.g., “Brand‑System Package – $5,000”). Highlight ROI (e.g., "A cohesive brand can lift conversion by 10 %").\n3. **Offer a “Starter” Package** – $2,500 for a limited‑scope audit and mood‑board; use it as a foot‑in‑the‑door to larger contracts.\n\n### Weeks 9‑12 – Close & Scale\n1. **Close First Corporate Deal** – Prioritize the prospect that has shown the most engagement. Use a short contract (HelloSign) and request a 30‑day payment term.\n2. **Document the Process** – After delivery, create a short case study (with client permission) to add to your portfolio.\n3. **Referral Loop** – Ask the new client for 2‑3 referrals. Offer a discount on the next project for each successful referral.\n4. **Review & Iterate** – At the end of month 3, assess numbers: emails sent, replies, proposals, closed deals. Adjust target list size or messaging accordingly.\n\n### Quick‑Reference Checklist (Print & Keep)\n- ✅ Portfolio audit completed\n- ✅ New corporate‑ready showcase live\n- ✅ LinkedIn & website refreshed\n- ✅ Target list of 50 companies compiled\n- ✅ 15 cold emails sent daily\n- ✅ Weekly blog/newsletter published\n- ✅ One corporate contract signed\n- ✅ Referral pipeline set up\n\nBy following this timeline, you’ll systematically shift your perception from a local freelancer to a credible B2B design partner, increasing both the caliber of projects and your monthly earnings. --> Text length need to be between 0 and 5000 characters

